Fine-tuning clearly helps. Raw CER drops from 0.567 to 0.129. The 8.6% vs 9.9% case-insensitive figures show capitalization isn’t the reason the baseline does so poorly.
Vocabulary scoring alone gets the pretrained model to 76%. That is a substantial part of the 96.7%, so the paper should present vocabulary scoring and fine-tuning as two contributions. Fine-tuning adds about 20 points on top of the vocabulary step (76.0% to 96.7%).
Run 1 and the augmented run are not separable on this test set. Their intervals overlap in every row (for example 96.5% [95.1, 97.8] vs 96.7% [95.4, 97.8]), so I would not claim augmentation improved the result. It did reduce overfitting (train loss no longer near zero), which you can report separately.
The intervals are optimistic. The bootstrap treats images as independent, and there are 60 per name from a limited handwriting pool.
The vocabulary-scored CER of 0.025 isn’t a character-reading measure. A wrong pick is a whole wrong name, so it mostly reflects the 3.3% of wrong picks.
A pipeline difference to mention. The zero-shot row uses EasyOCR’s own recognize() preprocessing, with its default contrast adjustment. The scored rows use the plain preprocessing from the trainer. That’s fair for a baseline, but state it in the methods.
These numbers still include the mislabeled rows. Fix them first, then re-run before reporting final figures.
Your model does not transfer to these photos. Raw CER is 0.68 here, against 0.13 on its own test set. The 96.7% figure holds only for images that look like your training data (clean scanned word crops). That gap is a legitimate finding, and the paper should report it as such, in a table that shows both in-distribution and out-of-distribution results.
Fine-tuning still helps, since raw CER falls from 0.979 to 0.679 against the off-the-shelf model. The vocabulary step helps a lot too (5.7% to 43.7%).
The uncertainty is large. 43.7% of 87 images is roughly ±10 points on its own, and more once you account for several photos of the same names. The A-vs-B difference (4 images) is noise.
Vocab A is the optimistic case, since the right name is always in the list.